# Default-deny inbound (security policy §5). Loaded by iptables-restore.service
# (enabled via the wants/ symlink in this overlay). Outbound stays open — this
# is a workstation, not a server. SSH is allowed because the live image runs
# sshd; tighten or drop the rule after installation if the host policy says so.
#
# ORDERING vs docker, and why it must not be "fixed" by hand: iptables-restore
# REPLACES the whole filter table, so it has to run before docker.service or it
# wipes the DOCKER/DOCKER-USER/DOCKER-FORWARD chains and containers lose
# networking until dockerd is restarted. Boot ordering gives that for free
# (measured on nvhard: restore 16:57:52, docker 16:57:59). To change rules on a
# RUNNING system use 'iptables -I/-D' against the live table, never a restore.
#
# This is the legacy xtables backend (CONFIG_*_IPTABLES_LEGACY, live.fragment).
# 'nft list ruleset' prints NOTHING here even though the firewall is fully
# active — check with 'iptables -S'. That mistake was made during the
# 2026-08-13 audit of the installed host and reported as "no firewall at all".
*filter
:INPUT DROP [0:0]
:FORWARD DROP [0:0]
:OUTPUT ACCEPT [0:0]
-A INPUT -i lo -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
# Container -> host. Docker manages FORWARD and nat, never INPUT, so without
# these two lines every container->host connection dies on the policy. Not
# hypothetical on these images: aillama binds llama-server to the docker0
# address and the Open WebUI container reaches it there (8080/8093/8094), and
# airag's embedding/rerank servers work the same way.
# Verified on nvhard 2026-08-13: a listener on 10.20.0.1:18093 was unreachable
# from a container until these rules existed. An earlier test against port 22
# wrongly looked fine — the ssh rule below is not interface-scoped, so it let
# container traffic through on that one port and hid the problem.
# br-+ covers docker's user-defined networks (br-<netid>, e.g. ollama-net).
# Blanket accept from the container bridges: any container may reach any host
# port. Deliberate for a single-user workstation running its own images; on a
# host running untrusted containers, narrow this to the specific ports.
-A INPUT -i docker0 -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -i br-+ -j ACCEPT
-A INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT
# Brute-force brake on sshd, per SOURCE ADDRESS (--hashlimit-mode srcip), so
# one noisy peer cannot lock anyone else out. Thresholds chosen to be invisible
# to legitimate use and still fatal to a scanner: burst 30 lets a script open
# thirty connections back-to-back, then the sustained rate is 15/minute. Human
# use and rsync/scp loops never approach that; password-guessing runs at
# hundreds per minute and starts hitting the DROP within seconds.
# REQUIRES CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_MATCH_HASHLIMIT (live.fragment). Without the
# module iptables-restore fails on this line and the machine boots with NO
# firewall — see the coupling note at that symbol.
-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -m hashlimit --hashlimit-name ssh --hashlimit-mode srcip --hashlimit-above 15/minute --hashlimit-burst 30 -j DROP
-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW -j ACCEPT
COMMIT
